## Part C — Groq models, re-verified against the live catalog (Sept 10, 2026)

You asked me to check current models rather than trust memory — I did, against `console.groq.com/docs/models` directly, and the picture has shifted slightly even since your Phase 1 plan was written a few days ago:

- **`llama-3.1-8b-instant` and `llama-3.3-70b-versatile` are now listed as Enterprise-only ("Contact Sales")** on Groq's production model table — not just deprecated, now gated behind an enterprise plan entirely. Do not plan around these as a free-tier fallback anywhere in your code (worth double-checking nothing in your codebase still references them as a fallback string).
- **`openai/gpt-oss-120b`** and **`openai/gpt-oss-20b`** remain the standard production text models, openly available, with Developer-plan limits of 250K TPM / 1,000 RPM (free tier is lower — roughly 30 RPM, ~8,000 TPM per Groq's public free-tier table — plenty for a hackathon demo, but don't hammer it during dev with your demo key).
- **`qwen/qwen3.6-27b`** is the vision-capable model currently on Groq's catalog (Preview tier — fine for a hackathon, not for anything you'd call production), supporting image input up to 20 MB per file, 250K TPM / 1,000 RPM on the Developer plan. **This is what `document_extractor.py`'s `MODEL_VISION` should actually point to** — the current default (`llama-3.2-11b-vision-preview`) does not appear on Groq's live catalog at all anymore and will fail outright if called.
- **`groq/compound`** and **`groq/compound-mini`** are still available — Groq's own agentic systems with built-in web search and code execution (200K TPM / 200 RPM). Still a legitimate shortcut for Scheme Agent if you want retrieval-augmented ranking without building a custom search tool, though your current implementation doesn't use this and doesn't need to for Phase 4.
- No new deprecations affect `openai/gpt-oss-120b`/`20b` since your Phase 1 plan was written — the model routing recommendation from that plan still holds. The one concrete change you need to make is the vision model ID.

